96
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
In this week's podcast in Scout Mastership, in Seven Minutes or less

97
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
we'll have our third installment of the Patrol Methods series

98
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
And then we've got some email questions to answer

99
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,000
and that constitutes the remainder of the podcast. ladies and gentlemen,

100
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Yes, it does. So let's get started, shall we?

101
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Scout Mastership in Seven Minutes or less.

102
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
So a couple of weeks ago we started talking about the Patrol Method

103
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:24,000
And our first installment talked about the Patrol Method as the character school of scouting,

104
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:29,000
That real self-government makes the scout oath and law more relevant than a bunch of concepts

105
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
preached by adults, and that scouts find meaning in the life of the patrol and troop,

106
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,000
where individual responsibilities kind of become group responsibilities.

107
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:42,000
It's the heart of the scouting movement, the Patrol Method.

108
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
And then last week we talked about the adult role

109
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:50,000
And you'll remember we kind of sketched out the idea of the adult role in the Patrol Method

110
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
being more responsive than directive.

111
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,000
And when we start talking about those things, a lot of people find that applying the Patrol Method

112
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
maybe has not been the strongest aspect of their troop

113
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And they find that there's some pretty dramatic changes that are needed.

114
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
And of course it's pretty predictable what happens when you suggest that things should change.

115
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,000
People are human beings and there's a lot of us that don't really appreciate changes

116
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
and we don't want to make changes.

117
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
So we raise some objections to those changes,

118
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And these objections to the Patrol Method come from adults and sometimes from scouts.

119
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
So discussing these objections is going to help us understand the Patrol Method a little better

120
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,000
And will lead us into the fourth and final installment of this series,

121
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
which is how to make the Patrol Method happen.

122
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
But right now let's concentrate on what the general objections are

123
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and see if that helps you understand things a little better.

124
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:51,000
The first one we usually run into is that if scouts are given just the free will choice

125
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:56,000
to form their own patrols, that their patrols aren't going to be all the same number,

126
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
They're not going to be balanced by age and rank and things like that.

127
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,000
And you're right,

128
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:05,000
If it's left up to a scout to decide who's in what patrol, there's not a whole lot of likelihood

129
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:10,000
They're going to be as well balanced as they would be if adults sat down with all the metrics

130
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:11,000
and figured them out.

131
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
But I'll tell you something: they will be much more effective

132
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Now. an ideal patrol has around eight scouts.

133
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
That's been the rule of thumb for about a century,

134
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:27,000
But there's no hard and fast rule that says a patrol can't be five or a patrol can't be ten,

135
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Eight just seems to be the best workable number.

136
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Some scouts do better when they're in a group of five.

137
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
Some scouts do better when they're in a group of ten.

138
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:41,000
If we let them decide, they're going to find the level that works best for them.

139
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:47,000
I do know that if a scout isn't in a patrol with his friends, he's going to find a way to be with his friends.

140
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
He'll gravitate towards them during meetings and outings and camping trips anyway.

141
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:59,000
A balanced patrol is one of those things that we talked about in past podcasts on the subject

142
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
as kind of an administrative decoration. that seems to matter a lot to adults.

143
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:11,000
But scouts, they're not so interested in balanced patrols as they are in patrols that work for them

144
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
and that have their friends in them.

145
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Because why do you join scouts? To do cool stuff with your friends.

146
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:22,000
If you join scouts and you find out that all your friends are over in two different other patrols,

147
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
you know what friendship is stronger than patrol membership.

148
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
You're going to find a way to hang out with your friends

149
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
And that's going to be frustrating for everybody involved.

150
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:38,000
So, yes, if, given the choice, scouts are not going to form the same balanced patrols that adults would form,

151
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,000
but the patrols that they do form are going to be much more effective.

152
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:52,000
The second big objection is that if we as adults don't have some kind of control or, say, over the process,

153
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
the scouts aren't going to elect qualified leaders.

154
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:00,000
And that can be true, but usually they'll make really good choices.

155
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
That's been my experience.

156
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:10,000
When somebody raises this objection to me, what I tell them is: the choice of the scouts is their choice in that matter right,

157
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
And we will work with their choices.

158
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:19,000
If they choose someone who we think is unqualified, well then it's up to the patrol leaders council,

159
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:24,000
the senior patrol leader and the adults involved to help them get up to speed

160
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
and to do their best.

161
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
We will meet the challenge no matter what.

162
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,000
The third objection I hear most often is: without a lot of direct adult oversight,

163
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:40,000
like an assistant scout master assigned to every patrol to make sure the patrol leader does everything that he's supposed to,

164
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
that scouts will just not do any of their work or take any responsibility.

165
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Scouts will take responsibility and they will do the job as they understand it.

166
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Our work is helping them understand

167
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:58,000
If adults are clearly in control, regardless of what scouts do or adults will do the work for them,

168
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
then scouts aren't going to do much. They're pretty smart that way.

169
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:08,000
If the adults are going to take over and do stuff and we can just kind of hang out, hey, that's no problem for me.

170
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
That makes my life a little bit simpler, a little bit easier,

171
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
And that's fine. you know,

172
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Makes sense.

173
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Now, when adults leave the scouts alone and let them get things done,

174
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
when they stop interfering or fixing or taking over,

175
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
scouts gradually learn how to make things happen.

176
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:32,000
The fourth objection is that if scouts don't plan meetings or camp outs very well

177
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:37,000
or present things at meetings as well as we do, our program is going to suffer.

178
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Well, if your goal is simply: you know, a quality show and earning lots of badges,

179
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
put the adults in charge because they do a much better job.

180
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Now the scouts really don't end up having to do much other than listen and follow along.

181
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
but you'll have good presentations and you'll probably have a lot of advancement.

182
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:00,000
But our goal really is not that kind of decorative quality of the program, is it,

183
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Or the number of badges that scouts earn.

184
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:09,000
It's providing the opportunity for scouts to learn about planning and presenting things and leading

185
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and to derive all the benefits of the patrol method.

186
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
Our goal is in building something for our scouts.

187
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:19,000
It's providing them the opportunity to build something for themselves.

188
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Now listen, if you're a parent, somewhere on your desk, on your dresser,

189
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,000
somewhere, you have this treasured object-

190
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:32,000
maybe a drawing or a lopsided clay statue- that your child created and gave to you.

191
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,000
That little piece of artwork is never going to be written about by art critics

192
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
or displayed in a national museum,

193
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,000
But to you it's a priceless piece of work. right?

194
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
We ought to use this perspective when we view what our scouts do.

195
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:49,000
Comparing their skills in planning and presenting things

196
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:54,000
and running meetings and all of that kind of stuff to what adults are capable of

197
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:59,000
is like holding that prized piece of artwork your child made up next to the Mona Lisa.

198
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
They're two totally different worlds, right?

199
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
If we value our scouts' effort above our own,

200
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:09,000
we'll begin to see the true value of the patrol system at work.

201
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,000
If we concentrate on making the patrol method a reality,

202
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:17,000
our scouts will get more out of scouting than just a lot of badges

203
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:23,000
or the pleasant memory of really cool presentations or activities that were led by adults.

204
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:28,000
The fifth objection is that if we give scouts the choice of what they're going to do

205
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
and their program. they will not advance.

206
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,000
They will not do the things that scouts do,

207
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,000
They'll decide to do stupid things.

208
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
It's kind of like the red herring is always:

209
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,000
well, you know, if we told them they could do anything they wanted to do,

210
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
they'd just sit around and play video games or something like that. right,

211
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Well, you know, scouts advance when they do the things that scouts do,

212
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and the Patrol Leaders Council is focused on doing what scouts do.

213
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
So scouts are going to advance.

214
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:02,000
They may advance a little slower, a little faster than they do, right, when adults run things,

215
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:06,000
but advancement is really just only one indication of success.

216
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:11,000
And remember, we're not chasing indicators and we're not decorating our program.

217
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
We're doing the patrol method.

218
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
That's the most important thing.

219
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:20,000
We need to value the other indications of success at least equally.

220
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
Did scouts plan and present their own meeting?

221
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
That's a great indicator of success.

222
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Did they plan and carry out their own camping trip?

223
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
Another great indicator of success.

224
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Have they grown more competent and skilled as leaders?

225
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
Are they gaining an appreciation for responsibility?

226
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Those are indicators that are hard to put in numbers on a piece of paper,

227
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
but they're really, really important.

228
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:46,000
Our job is to give them real responsibility about making the choices about what they do.

229
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
We also work with them to keep it in the context of scouting.

230
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:53,000
And they're going to do what scouts do because really, you know,

231
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
that's why we're there in the first place. and they want to do what scouts do

232
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
because that's why they're there.

233
00:15:58,000 --> 00:15:59,000
They want to go camping,

234
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:00,000
They want to advance,

235
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
They want to learn the skills of scouting.

236
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,000
That objection is really reasonably easy to overcome.

237
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:12,000
Objection number six is our older scouts don't want to work with younger scouts.

238
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:15,000
I'll agree that that is the case a lot of times.

239
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
but I will say this: football players,

240
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:23,000
they're not particularly fond of wind, sprints and drills in practice,

241
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
but eventually they see the value of them.

242
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Older scouts aren't always excited about working with younger scouts,

243
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,000
but they reap the same benefits of service that we adults experience.

244
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:34,000
They will get it.

245
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:40,000
This can be a particularly frustrating issue in a troop that runs the patrol method,

246
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:45,000
because you know we're hands off and we're out observing what's going on

247
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
rather than trying to control what's going on,

248
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
And we will see the older scouts kind of grouped over here

249
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
and the younger scouts kind of grouped over there every once in a while.

250
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:58,000
Well, what you're going to do and what your job is in that situation

251
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:03,000
is to build in them, build in those older scouts, an ideal of service,

252
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:06,000
And it can be a big challenge, but they get it and they will follow up with it.

253
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
While you're building this ideal of service,

254
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:13,000
you also have to recognize the idea that they want to be with their peers

255
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
and not just with all the younger guys all the time.

256
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,000
So you build in opportunities for that to happen too.

257
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:24,000
And finally, a lot of the objection comes down to listen.

258
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:30,000
we're just not comfortable with giving scouts this kind of responsibility and power.

259
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
We just think everything's going to fall apart.

260
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
So go back to the sports analogy for a moment.

261
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
Are coaches afraid to give their players too much power?

262
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
When a team takes to the basketball court and the coach stays on the sidelines

263
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
while the players play the game,

264
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,000
the coach isn't worried about the player, that the players are going to start playing soccer

265
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
or lacrosse on the basketball court- right,

266
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Because he's taken the time to train the players in the rules and the skills of the game.

267
00:17:54,000 --> 00:18:00,000
And once again, we guide and train our scouts to lead themselves in the context of scouting.

268
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:05,000
So there are rules and goals to the game that we help them understand.

269
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
The rules are the scout oath and law.

270
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:11,000
the goals are forming character-driven, contributing members of our community.

271
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:15,000
And what you have to do is you have to trust that the patrol method

272
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:19,000
is a process that is going to create exactly that:

273
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Character-driven, contributing members of the community.

274
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
And it's a process,

275
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
It's not a switch that we can throw on or off.

276
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:31,000
We can't wave a magic wand and all of a sudden have the patrol method fully active,

277
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:36,000
fully capable and looking exactly like we've pictured it in our minds.

278
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,000
It's a challenge, but it's one of the most rewarding and greatest challenges of scouting.

279
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
So how are we going to make it happen?

280
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:48,000
What are the practical logistic considerations in putting the patrol method into practice?

281
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
How does it actually work on the ground?

282
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Well, in our fourth and final installment on the patrol method,

283
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
that's exactly what we're going to talk about:

284
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:37,000
E-mail, that is, folks,

285
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:41,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

286
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:51,000
I have three e-mail questions that came in

287
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:57,000
and they all touch on the principle of dealing with individual scouts as they come to us,

288
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:01,000
accepting them just as they are and working with them.

289
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:05,000
Some scouts come to us and they're a little troubled or they have some challenges.

290
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Our work is making scouting available to them in a way that they can get the most out of the whole experience.

291
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:16,000
Now there are policies and rules and regulations and procedures and loads of advice,

292
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:22,000
but when it comes down to it, we have to thoughtfully and carefully consider the individual scout,

293
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
their ambitions and their needs,

294
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:30,000
Not just the giant herd of scouts that we're working with, but each individual scout.

295
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:36,000
It's a balancing act and the answers to these three situations are definitely reflective of that.

296
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:42,000
We are sure to be successful when we call on our own sense of empathy, understanding and compassion

297
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:47,000
and we work hard to understand what's going on in the life of an individual scout.

298
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Each one of these situations deals with some delicate matters,

299
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:56,000
so we're going to withhold the names of the people who sent the e-mails in at their request,

300
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
But the first one says this:

301
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:02,000
I was filling in for our advancement chair while conducting a border review this week

302
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
and it was great fun, as usual.

303
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:11,000
One review was for a socially challenged but active scout with kind of one of those parents

304
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
who's like a helicopter parent.

305
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:17,000
I received an interesting e-mail from our scout master afterwards

306
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:24,000
and he said I was a bit surprised that the scout in question passed the border review.

307
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,000
I completed a scout master conference just before

308
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,000
and I really didn't expect him to complete the border review.

309
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:33,000
In my opinion, he may not have been ready to advance,

310
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:37,000
although he did have all the requirements completed and signed off in his book.

311
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:40,000
During our conference he barely spoke at all,

312
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:45,000
but he did tell me that he was just looking for the easiest way to get out of work

313
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
in completing his requirements.

314
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
I want to help him and not stand in the way,

315
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:53,000
but frankly, I just really believe that he's not ready to advance

316
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
if he has that kind of attitude.

317
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:58,000
If you guys on the border review saw something different,

318
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:01,000
I'd love to hear about it so I can work with it.

319
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:04,000
And the person who sat on the board answered back.

320
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:08,000
I was impressed that this scout asked to have his border review

321
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
because his mother was nowhere in sight.

322
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:15,000
Yes, I understand exactly what you're saying there.

323
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:19,000
The poor kid sat there and shook during the entire border review,

324
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:23,000
but he made eye contact and gave more than yes or no answers

325
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
and formed full sentences,

326
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:30,000
which was a considerable improvement over his past border review.

327
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
Was he as sharp as his fellow scouts?

328
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
No, but that's really not here or there.

329
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:38,000
We don't want to compare scouts to one or another in a border review.

330
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
We want to see them growing individually,

331
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
And so our judgment during a border review

332
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:48,000
is based on what the individual progress that particular scout has made.

333
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:53,000
I think we also have to consider that his parents are a little over-involved

334
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
and that colors his experience,

335
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
And, of course, none of us can really pick our parents.

336
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:02,000
We have to look past the kind of pressure that gets exerted by ambitious parents

337
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
to the interests of the scout.

338
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,000
We aim to provide a positive opportunity for him to discover

339
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:13,000
and develop his own ambitions rather than those being imposed on him by a parent.

340
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:17,000
We must be careful not to misdirect the frustration

341
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:21,000
of dealing with a really ambitious parent onto the scout,

342
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
And that's a challenge for all of us, isn't it?

343
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:28,000
At least the parent we're talking about is aware that they get a little over-involved

344
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
and they're struggling to let go at times,

345
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:34,000
And we ought to be thankful that they are at least aware of the situation,

346
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:37,000
because a lot of helicopter parents are completely oblivious

347
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:40,000
to the difficulties that they cause for their scout,

348
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
And I thought that was a very good answer.

349
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:47,000
We're focused on the individual scout, not how he does in comparison with others,

350
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
and that's the real genius of scouting.

351
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:53,000
the awkward, uncommunicative or even treacherous and reluctant scout

352
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:58,000
who's being pushed by a parent is going to have kind of a bad attitude,

353
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:03,000
And it's a great example of why we need to look for things in each individual scout

354
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:06,000
and not compare them to everybody else.

355
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:09,000
We do what we can to focus on his best interests.

356
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
We aren't hurting sheep right.

357
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
We're dealing with one boy at a time.

358
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:17,000
And then I had this question from a cubmaster

359
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:20,000
The last night. a question came up regarding a cub's brother

360
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
who wants to participate in our pack.

361
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:25,000
The younger brother is a wolf and his older brother,

362
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:28,000
who wants to participate, has Down syndrome.

363
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:32,000
As a pack, we're open and accepting and we want this child to feel welcome.

364
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:36,000
we also want him to be awarded for his accomplishments, as he does his best.

365
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,000
I'm not too sure how to best handle the situation.

366
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:44,000
Would we be required to assign him to an official den based on his age,

367
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
or can he participate in the same den with his older brother

368
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
even though he's a couple years older?

369
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:53,000
My advice is that when you're dealing with a scout with special needs,

370
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:59,000
I suggest that probably the best idea is to kind of reverse engineer the experience.

371
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
Start with the scout and his family.

372
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:06,000
What's the ideal arrangement that delivers the promises of scouting to them?

373
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:09,000
Now, it may take a little while to figure that out,

374
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:13,000
but work along with the family and don't wait until you have everything figured out.

375
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:17,000
Look at the scout participating and because that is going to help you

376
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,000
figure out exactly what's going to work best for them.

377
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:24,000
Now, once you've worked this out with his family and you define the experience,

378
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:29,000
the next step is taking that definition and fitting all the policies and procedures to it.

379
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:33,000
You know, I kind of picture a wiring diagram where you're engineering an adapter plug.

380
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,000
I mean I hope that makes sense.

381
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:40,000
You'll find that there's a lot of leeway to define things for special needs scouts

382
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:44,000
and someone within your council knows exactly how to do that.

383
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
You'll want to locate them.

384
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:50,000
I would give your district executive a call, or just cold call the council office

385
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:55,000
and ask who can help out explaining some of the ins and outs of the way that this works.

386
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:58,000
Like I said, it's very flexible.

387
00:25:58,000 --> 00:25:59,000
There's a lot of leeway.

388
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:02,000
At one point you'll be making a decision

389
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:08,000
whether the scouting question is going to go with advancement requirements as they are in the book

390
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:12,000
or if they're going to go with a set of alternative requirements, and

391
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:16,000
this is one of the things that you and the family are going to work out together.

392
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:20,000
But that's only the advancement part and that's not the most important part, is it?

393
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:26,000
The most important part is that we have a child who's able to get the benefit of being in scouting.

394
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:31,000
What you and his family define as an appropriate method for evaluation for advancement,

395
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:36,000
and what age group he participates with will likely work

396
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:42,000
when it comes to making that adaptation for officialdom.

397
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:47,000
If he's a couple years older and he wants to be with his brother and that works out best,

398
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
usually that's not going to be a problem

399
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:53,000
And, like I said, you'll make a decision based on his abilities:

400
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:57,000
whether he can be assessed with the requirements in the book

401
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:01,000
or whether it's going to make more sense to have a set of alternate requirements.

402
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:08,000
And thirdly, in this triumvirate of emails that all deal with working with individual scouts,

403
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:09,000
I got this message.

404
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:12,000
I listened to your podcast avidly and thank you for all you do.

405
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:13,000
It's helping me.

406
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:17,000
I learned that a scout in my troop had overdosed on a drug.

407
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:22,000
We've really had no issues with his involvement in the troop.

408
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:25,000
All of this happened outside of scouting.

409
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:29,000
I was very concerned about this and I reached out to my district executive

410
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:35,000
to see whether this type of conduct would prevent him from continuing as a member of our troop

411
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:41,000
or from advancing, potentially, to Eagle, because the scout in question is a life scout.

412
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:46,000
The district executive said he really couldn't see anything disqualifying in this incident

413
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
as it was explained to him.

414
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:53,000
Now, that being said, it's going to be a source of some discussion amongst parents and leaders

415
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:55,000
in our troop and I want to be prepared.

416
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:01,000
The scout appears to be okay, but he's clearly troubled and he's been in distress for some time.

417
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:04,000
I don't want to turn my back on him in his time of trouble,

418
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:11,000
but I also don't want to get crosswise of any principles or policies that I'm supposed to be observing as a scout leader.

419
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:13,000
Have you ever experienced anything like this?

420
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:15,000
Thanks for your help.

421
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:21,000
Well, the scout you've told me about, like any other young person, needs an intact support network,

422
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:23,000
and scouting is going to be part of that.

423
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:26,000
He's going through some tough times, to be sure,

424
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:31,000
but he hasn't really threatened the safety or well-being of anyone other than himself, right?

425
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:36,000
And the idea of having a drug overdose, even though it happened outside of scouting.

426
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:41,000
well, that is naturally something that would raise a lot of concern.

427
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:46,000
But other than expressing that concern to him directly and offering any support to him personally-

428
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:51,000
and, you know, making sure that this is not an ongoing problem-

429
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:54,000
I don't see any other actions that need to be addressed.

430
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:58,000
Maintaining that intact support network for one of our children

431
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:02,000
who is going through tough times is very, very important.

432
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:05,000
I wouldn't want to go in with a lot of value judgments and things like that.

433
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:10,000
I would want to make sure that they understood we were there to help them as best we can

434
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:13,000
and to keep that support network intact.

435
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:17,000
Now, when and if he ends up in front of an Eagle border review,

436
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:21,000
maybe he'll be asked to explain some of these things.

437
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:25,000
Maybe there'll be a time where you sit down and have a scout master's conference

438
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:29,000
and you ask him to explain these things in the light of the scout oath and law

439
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
and you help him figure his way through them.

440
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:35,000
And you know, if you do this wisely and you're a compassionate counselor,

441
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:40,000
you don't have to offer any judgments or be difficult with this scout.

442
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:45,000
You have an honest, open discussion with him in the context of the scout oath and law

443
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:48,000
and look for the conclusions that he comes to,

444
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:52,000
and I'll bet that you're going to have a lot of common ground.

445
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
Now. I've worked with plenty of scouts over time

446
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:58,000
who have encountered some major obstacle, personally

447
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:01,000
or in their family life, or at school or elsewhere.

448
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:06,000
And I'll say it again: what they all need is an intact support network

449
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:09,000
and caring adults committed to their success and well-being.

450
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:13,000
Now it sounds like, from the sense of the question that you asked me,

451
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:15,000
that you're one of those people.

452
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:18,000
So follow your heart and continue to be there for this scout.

453
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:21,000
Not everyone will think that this is a good idea

454
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:25,000
and you may have to explain yourself and not everybody may get on board,

455
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:31,000
But the point is is that you're doing your best to help a troubled individual scout,

456
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:34,000
